.TH FINGERPRINT 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fingerprint \- identify a Mach-O, and compare two of them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fingerprint
.I binary
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.br
.B fingerprint
.I a
.I b
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers
.RB \(lq "is this the same program as that one?" \(rq,
which is a different question from
.RB \(lq "are these the same file?" \(rq
and the one you actually have when comparing a build against a baseline.
.SH THREE QUESTIONS, THREE ANSWERS
.TP
.B uuid
the same
.IR build .
Exact, and useless the moment anything is relinked.
.TP
.B fingerprint
the same
.IR program .
Survives a rebuild; changes if a symbol or a section does.
.TP
.B structure
the same
.IR shape .
Weaker, and all a stripped binary can offer.
.PP
Every existing answer is a byte comparison, and byte comparison gets it wrong in
both directions. Two builds of one source differ in every address, because PIE and
ASLR move them; in the current-version fields of the dylibs they load; and in any
timestamp \- so
.B cmp
reports them different. Two
.I different
programs built from one template with a function renamed differ in almost nothing
structural \- so a loose structural diff reports them the same.
.SH WHAT IS EXCLUDED
Nothing that a rebuild moves enters the digest: no address, no size, no offset. Nor
do the provenance commands
.BR LC_UUID ,
.BR LC_CODE_SIGNATURE ,
.B LC_DYLIB_CODE_SIGN_DRS
or
.BR LC_SOURCE_VERSION ,
which record the build rather than the program. The UUID is reported separately and
exactly, which is a better home for it.
.PP
Section
.I sizes
are excluded too. That does make a stripped binary's fingerprint weaker, which is
why
.B tier
is reported rather than presenting a structure-only digest as though it were as
strong as a full one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
same program, or a single file, which always succeeds
.TP
.B 1
different programs \- a negative answer, not a failure
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
a file could not be read
.PP
A comparison where one side is stripped exits 0 on a structural match, so
.B caveat
says so. A gate that needs more than shape should compare UUIDs, which is what they
are for.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
fingerprint /usr/local/go/bin/go
fingerprint shipped.app/…/app baseline.app/…/app
fingerprint \-\-json a.dylib b.dylib | jq .data.verdict
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audit (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR describe (1)